# Operator prose for the summarization quality protocol (issue #893, parent #710).
#
# `src/summarization/validation/` owns the protocol -- which files are drawn,
# which criteria are scored, and when the ratchet holds. This file owns its
# words -- the published description of every criterion, every sentence the
# ratchet emits when it refuses a run, and the sentences `formal-ai
# summarization` prints. They used to be English literals inside the Rust
# modules, which R379 forbids -- no hardcoded natural language in `src/`.
#
# The `{...}` fields are filled from the measured run. The criterion names
# (`identity_names_path`, `compression`) stay language-neutral, so the committed
# baseline and the report parser never have to read prose.
#
# Every supported language carries every intent, the same parity the other
# `multilingual-responses-*.lino` files are held to. The CLI and the CI gate
# read the `en` record (`PUBLICATION_LANGUAGE` in
# `src/summarization/validation/prose.rs`), because the report is published in
# English next to the case study in docs/case-studies/issue-893/; the other
# languages are here so a reader in one of them gets the same protocol, and so
# adding a language means adding records here, not editing Rust.
multilingual_responses
